From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 08:51:53 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] um: apply only changed kernel PTEs in a Darwin kernel TLB
 sync

A kernel-range walk mapped every present PTE of its range again. A
vmalloc purge flushes the whole span between the areas it frees, and
the interpreted BPF programs a seccomp sandbox loads and frees purge on
every free (vm_reset_perms()), so most host map calls of such a walk
re-created a mapping the host already had.

Clear a kernel PTE's _PAGE_NEEDSYNC by compare and exchange once its
host mapping is applied, and skip a kernel PTE, or a 4 KiB profile
slot none of whose quarters changed, that does not carry the bit. A
writer that changes the PTE meanwhile has set the bit again and the
flush it publishes applies the new value. User PTEs keep the bit.

The exact repair of a kernel-address fault sets the bit on the
faulting page before its walk, so it still applies that page.

A guest loop of fork bursts whose children exit at once, where the
vfree of their vmapped stacks purges a vmap range every few dozen
rounds, ran 38% faster on 4 KiB pages and 42% faster on 16 KiB pages
(4 vCPUs).
